/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<CLIFF_GENERATION>
  min_number_of_cliffs 20
  max_number_of_cliffs 20
  min_length_of_cliff 2
  max_length_of_cliff 4
  cliff_curliness 10
  min_distance_cliffs 4

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

  create_object FORAGE
  {
    number_of_objects 25
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_45c - arm 3 of 3. Minimum below the floor, maximum above it.
Feeds: preview-design Sec.6.3, cliffs.ts, Sec.15 item 17(b)

READ RMSTEST_45b's HEADER FIRST. It carries the full question and the reading
rule for all three arms.

THIS ARM: minimum 2, maximum 4. It straddles the documented floor, which is the
only arm that can distinguish a whole-section suppression from a per-draw yield.
A partial count here - roughly a third to a half of 45b - is the per-draw
reading, and it is the outcome that neither the guide nor the spec anticipates.

This is also the shape the shipped map cited in the item actually writes: five
to ten cliffs at length two to four.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. TWO RUNS.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --clusters
*/
